{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfdunaSUYTpviLRWmptFX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote 8 test statistics for the incumbent `personal_pattern_detection_gain` into `iter_4/critique/test_stats/`. I ran each one once on the observed `responses.csv`: each returned a finite float in 0.05 s or less, well under the 0.3 s per-call budget. I didn't run the posterior-predictive check itself, as instructed, so there are no p-values yet.\n\n| Statistic | What it probes | Observed |\n|---|---|---|\n| `participant_majority_agreement_variance` | Re-test of the one discrepancy that survived the multiple-comparison correction last round: do people still differ more than predicted in how often they agree with the majority? This model's per-person \"noticing structure\" trait was added to fix it. | 0.0260 |\n| `participant_side_bias_variance` | How much people differ in how often they press Left. This has come up in every round so far, and the model has no left/right term. | 0.0055 |\n| `split_half_side_bias_correlation` | Whether a person's Left rate on odd trials matches their rate on even trials, i.e. whether the side bias is a stable personal trait or just noise. | 0.002 |\n| `agreement_side_bias_interaction` | Across people, the correlation between agreeing with the majority and leaning to one side. A negative reading would mean low-agreement people guess by pressing one button. The model's guessing is unbiased. | 0.091 |\n| `near_unanimous_pair_fraction` | Share of pairs (with at least 10 responses) where 85% or more of people picked the same sequence. Tests whether the model's guessing rate is too high or too low on easy pairs. | 0.247 |\n| `short_minus_long_pair_consensus` | How decisive people are on short pairs (2–5 flips) compared with 8-flip pairs. The model scales its features by sequence length, so it may get this wrong. | 0.077 |\n| `over_alternation_zone_choice` | When both sequences switch between H and T at a rate of 0.6 or more, how often people pick the one that switches more. Tests how lenient the model is toward too much switching. | 0.335 |\n| `alternation_choice_perseveration` | Whether a choice of the more-switching sequence makes the same choice more likely on the next trial. The model treats every trial as independent. | 0.131 |\n\n`alternation_choice_perseveration` can only be read against the model's replicates. If the trial order puts similar pairs next to each other, they push the observed value up too, but the replicates use the same pairs in the same order, so that effect should cancel out."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":666,"cache_read_input_tokens":53843,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":666,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7e3ce975-7510-4e79-8c7d-dd513882f53c","uuid":"c1f44831-fbb9-4f1b-85d3-c9ef6e63b71a","timestamp":"2026-10-02T18:49:10.564Z","request_id":"req_011CfdunZWfxpDJfrKVxi4Jo"}
